I just find out the sky modification in this PR is NOT needed. All these modification is to apply a small depth to screen. The same thing can be achieved with a custom post processing gdshader. You can draw a full screen quad that adjusts depth following the [tutorial](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/tutorials/shaders/advanced_postprocessing.html#full-screen-quad). I put my working shader below in case anyone is interested.

```glsl
shader_type spatial;
render_mode unshaded, fog_disabled, depth_draw_always, blend_mix;

#define DEPTH_BIAS 0.00000001

void vertex() {
	POSITION = vec4(VERTEX.xy, DEPTH_BIAS, 1.0);
}

void fragment() {
	ALBEDO = vec3(0,0,0);
	ALPHA = 0.0;
}
```

This is a more general solution. It also works for background with clear color. It does not require any engine source change.

Now I think all problems introduced in this PR have a proper solution.

1. Fields/APIs introduced specifically for MTLRasterizationRateMap in forward mobile renderer

I pushed a PR https://github.com/rsanchezsaez/godot/pull/2 that encapsulates MTLRasterizationRateMap as a special kind of VRS texture. All ad hoc methods and fields like `get_viewport_for_view`, `xr_viewports`, `rasterization_rate_map` can be removed.

2. Sky shader variant introduced for depth write

This is absolutely unnecessary.  As user can already do the same thing with custom post processing. Modification of sky in this PR should be reverted.

@BastiaanOlij @huisedenanhai @dsnopek @CCranney Thanks a lot for the in-depth reviews, for the comments detailing your experiments, and for all the details! I agree with the desire to have as little platform specific code as possible in the common rendering path, and I also agree about trying not to have `XRInterface` API changes that only work for a single platform.

@huisedenanhai Thanks for providing a PR with some of the suggested improvements!

I'll take a deeper look at all the provided details and code this coming week, and I'll strive to provide an updated PR soon. I'll also look into splitting smaller independent improvements such as new warnings into separate PRs.

@huisedenanhai Thanks for all your detailed experiments, all your comments, and for your PR. I looked at your patch, and it looks great! It's a very good approach to avoid adding visionOS specific logic to the main render path, and to `XRInterface`.

However, I ran into a small problem. When running a visionOS immersive project with your patch in debug mode, the project breakpoints in this `DEV_ASSERT`, and the `p_usage` parameter is `RESOURCE_USAGE_NONE`. When running in release, everything seems to work correctly, because I assume `DEV_ASSERT` is disabled.

```
bool RenderingDeviceGraph::_is_write_usage(ResourceUsage p_usage) {
	switch (p_usage) {
                        // ...
			DEV_ASSERT(false && "Invalid resource tracker usage.");
			return false;
	}
}
```

I tried setting these spoofed flags to the fake texture wrapping the `rasterizationRateMap`, by mimicking the flags that are set by the `OpenXR` module:

```
texture.usage_flags = RD::TEXTURE_USAGE_COLOR_ATTACHMENT_BIT | RD::TEXTURE_USAGE_SAMPLING_BIT | RD::TEXTURE_USAGE_STORAGE_BIT | RD::TEXTURE_USAGE_VRS_ATTACHMENT_BIT;
```

but that didn't help.

Do you know a good way of fixing this issue?

<img width="1557" height="1049" alt="Screenshot 2025-10-30 at 9 50" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/8e96ce58-48a8-4e13-9532-b23d776ec33d" />

Apologies for the extended delay in updating this PR. I expect we'll be able to iterate on this PR in a quicker manner from now on. 

@huisedenanhai Thanks so much for your suggestions, and your proposed solutions. I have pushed a new version of this PR adopting most of your proposed `rasterizationRateMap` handling. I have also listed you as a co-author at the description at the top of the PR.

@BastiaanOlij @dsnopek I agree with @huisedenanhai that `rasterizationRateMap` is a useful feature for all Apple platforms, and hence it's in Godot's benefit to have some explicit official support as part of the renderer. I think the solution of supplying it wrapped on an additional fake-texture attachment (with added logic to inform the renderer to ignore this texture for texture operations), and storing it on the `MDFramebuffer` object is reasonable. Let me know if disagree, and I'm happy to continue iterating it this (cc @clayjohn as well).

@BastiaanOlij With this push, I think we have addressed your big three areas of concern:
- HDR output changes have been reverted, and now we recommend enabling it on the xr intialization script.
- sky shader changes have been reverted, and we can come up with a separate solution for skybox/transparent content. Perhaps we can also note this https://github.com/godotengine/godot/pull/109975#issuecomment-3446873204 possible solution in the documentation.
- `rasterizationRateMap` handling now is cleaner, and all `XRInterface` changes have been reverted.

@CCranney Thank you for your experiments and additional details in testing this code.

@allenwp Thanks for your thoughts on HDR. Metal content rendered on visionOS through Compositor Services must be HDR, so in a way HDR cannot be turned off. For now, I think it's ok to not do anything special in this PR, and just recommend enabling `viewport.use_hdr_2d = true` on the init script. If after the HDR APIs for Apple platforms stabilize, there is a different preferred solution for visionOS, we can address separately.

I think I have addressed everybody comments. If anybody feels your comment was not sufficiently addressed, please feel free to respond again, and I'll do my best to address it and/or iterate further.

@Clancey @Nocxr Really cool to see you experimenting with it!

@dsnopek Thanks for the clarification. I'll have another look at the methods that can be accessed from `GDScript`.

@AThousandShips Apologies for mistakenly committing the file and for missing the disclosure. I have begun to use an internally approved version of Claude Code in January this year. I have used it to ask questions about the codebase, to research different possible solutions, and to assist with merge conflicts. Other than that, all the code has been written, reviewed, and tested by humans in our team. All the iterations of this PR prior to January were done without it.

@dsnopek I opened a docs PR with the gotchas from this thread: godotengine/godot-docs#12026

It expands the visionOS export page to cover immersive mode and the silent-failure pitfalls — `XROrigin3D.current = true`, Mobile renderer requirement, `XRCamera3D.near >= 0.1` (the one you hit, with the Console.app log), `VRS_XR` being mandatory, and the mixed-immersion depth/alpha rule with @huisedenanhai's depth-bias shader as the general fix. It also drops the now-stale "immersive not supported" note and fixes the dangling `doc_compiling_for_visionos` reference.

Sequenced to land **with this PR**, since it documents the APIs introduced here. Review very welcome — happy to adjust placement or wording.
